Tessera NEWS -- history of user-visible changes.

Copyright (C) 2026 Bingshan Chang.
See the end of the file for license conditions.

This file describes releases of Tessera, Tessera X, and Elfeed X.
See their manuals for configuration and Lisp interfaces.

* Changes in Elfeed X 0.1.0

** Extra features for Elfeed are available as an independent package.
Elfeed X requires Emacs 30.1 and Elfeed 4.0.1 or later.  The 'x' means extra.
It does not depend on Tessera.  Its features have independent global minor
modes; loading their libraries does not activate them.

** Scheduled updates avoid duplicate timers and overlapping retrieval.
'elfeed-x-auto-update-mode' starts a schedule on entry into Elfeed, or when
enabled with an existing Elfeed buffer.  The first attempt waits 900 seconds by
default.  Subsequent attempts use the same interval and skip busy queues.
Customize 'elfeed-x-update-interval' to restart an active schedule with a new
interval.  Disabling the mode cancels its timer without cancelling retrieval.

'elfeed-x-update-timer' and 'elfeed-x-next-update-time' expose the active timer
and its next planned attempt.  They return nil without an active timer.

** Search navigation can follow entries in an existing article window.
'elfeed-x-search-follow-mode' follows 'next-line' and 'previous-line', normally
bound to 'n' and 'p'.  It retains Search focus and point, marks the displayed
entry as read, and reuses a non-dedicated article window on the selected frame.
Point retains its column when marking an unread entry rebuilds the Search row.
Navigation alone does not open an article window.  Custom commands can be
added to 'elfeed-x-search-follow-commands'.

** WebKit widgets can fit the actual article window after rendering.
'elfeed-x-webkit-mode' adjusts widget dimensions without selecting the article
window.  Configure the optional 'elfeed-webkit' renderer and its bindings
separately.  The fitting mode has no effect without xwidget support.

* Changes in Tessera 0.1.1

** Shared header lines for native entry views.
Elfeed Search, Gnus Summary, and mu4e Headers display update actions,
query or source information, optional extra information, and statistics.
Each region has a replaceable function, independent faces, and hover help.
The update action invokes the native command.  Relative times can show the
last update or a known next update; Tessera does not schedule retrieval.

Elfeed distinguishes displayed entries from query results.  Gnus distinguishes
displayed articles from loaded headers.  Unread counts cover the displayed
set, including folded and off-screen entries.  Statistics respect the glyph
style and color settings, and long fields adapt to each window's width.
Header interior padding defaults to zero.  Adjacent fields and statistic
items use one separating space; displayed and total counts use '500/1284'.

The new headers are enabled with the existing display modes.  Set
'tessera-header-line-enabled' to nil to keep native headers while retaining
Tessera entry layouts.  Disabling a mode restores its native header settings.

Elfeed headers report feed parsing failures as failed updates.  Header
truncation accounts for the face of the ellipsis, including custom fonts
and fields containing different font sizes.  Feed update notifications visit
only registered Elfeed headers and combine changes during batch dispatch.
Elfeed update status remains active until all queued feeds finish.  Clearing
the native queue with 'elfeed-unjam' clears the pending header state.  Late
results from cancelled requests cannot change the status of a later update.

Mu4e query information follows the current Headers buffer.  Mail update and
context changes refresh headers without recounting unchanged message lists.

** Elfeed Search navigation works with continuous reading.
Tessera's 'n' and 'p' navigation works with 'elfeed-x-search-follow-mode'
without extra command configuration.  Marking an entry as read during
continuous reading preserves its highlight after the row is redrawn.
Individual entry updates retain each window's position independently and
preserve the mark even when it lies outside the narrowed region.

** Entry layouts follow buffer-local font scaling.
Gnus Summary, mu4e Headers, and Elfeed Search refresh cached entry widths
and truncation after font remapping changes, including
'text-scale-adjust'.  Thread counts and status slots share an aligned
prefix at each font size.  In graphical windows, entry width allocation
and truncation use measured text widths, including font scaling and mixed
faces.  Spacing options retain their character-column units.
Redrawing entries preserves point and mark within their entries and keeps
positions independent in each window displaying the buffer.  Elfeed also
preserves buffer narrowing when font changes redraw the entries.

** Interactive month groups in entry views.
Gnus Summary, mu4e Headers, and Elfeed Search entries are grouped beneath
month headings without copying or replacing native records.  Gnus temporarily
uses matching native date sorting while grouping is active; the prior sorting
and its buffer locality are restored when grouping is disabled.  Groups start
expanded and can be folded with mouse button 1.  A folded heading shows read
and unread record counts using the backend's configured status glyphs.

Native next and previous commands skip folded groups.  Commands that select a
hidden record reveal its group, as does Isearch.  The global thread-date
policy defaults to the latest reply.  Gnus can inherit or override that
policy.  Mu4e uses the latest reply; its 'inherit' setting falls back to
'latest' when the global policy is 'root'.  Native thread folding does not
change month assignment.

Missing or invalid dates remain in native order and receive a warning
background and inline warning glyph.  Fold state survives refreshes while
the buffer lives.  Global and per-backend options control month grouping,
spacing, faces, glyphs, title formatting, and thread date policy.

Elfeed retains explicitly customized native date separators.  Its native
default separator is hidden only while month grouping is active.

Month headings adapt independently to each window's width and account for
buffer-local font scaling.  Folding reuses the current groups, and Elfeed
navigation uses their cached entry positions.
Gnus and Elfeed batch month updates when a command changes several records.
Changing month title formatting or spacing redraws only the headings.
In narrowed buffers, folding keeps point within the accessible region and
leaves at least one accessible month expanded.

** Lower memory use when updating deep threads.
Gnus and mu4e share current thread paths across unchanged rows instead of
retaining earlier paths after successive message updates.

** Shared spacing option names.
'tessera-entry-safe-gap' is now 'tessera-safe-gap', and
'tessera-entry-flex-gap-min-width' is now 'tessera-flex-gap-min-width'.
Entries and month headings share these settings.  Because Tessera is still
below version 1.0, the old names are not retained as compatibility aliases.

** Elfeed row updates preserve narrowed views and reuse month groups.
Updating entries redraws their full-buffer rows even when Search is narrowed.
Point, mark, and narrowing boundaries follow their entries across the redraw.
When entry identity, date, and row length stay unchanged, updates reuse month
groups and refresh their counts and display without regrouping all entries.
Read-state updates are faster in large Search buffers, including when the
buffer is displayed in several windows.

* Changes in Tessera X 0.1.1

** Linked-page parsing has a separate input size limit.
'tessera-x-elfeed-fetch-max-bytes' defaults to 2097152 bytes (2 MiB).
Larger responses retain the stored feed body without decoding or HTML
parsing.  Set the option to nil to disable this limit.  The check occurs
after retrieval and does not limit the HTTP transfer or response buffer.

** Experimental context requests preserve newer work during completion.
A request cancelled or replaced by a mode hook or cleanup function no longer
publishes a stale result or invokes its ready hook.  Replacement requests and
previous successful snapshots remain available.  If the result buffer is
killed before publication while its request is still current, completion
fails instead of publishing a dead buffer.

For Lisp producers, 'tessera-x-context-start' now signals a user error if
cleanup cancels or supersedes the new request during startup.  A normal return
always supplies the source buffer's current pending request.

** Resource cleanup continues after errors and quits.
A cleanup error or quit no longer prevents attempts to release remaining
timers, subprocesses, and temporary buffers.  This applies to MIME extraction,
snapshot completion, mu index queries, and Elfeed linked-page retrieval.
Quitting during linked-page response processing cancels the pending request
and retains the previous successful snapshot.

** Missing and invalid dates no longer prevent snapshot preparation.
Snapshots display 'unknown' for unusable dates, including mu4e's no-date
marker, while preserving valid Unix epoch dates.  Elfeed's today command skips
entries without a usable timestamp instead of failing or changing their
stored dates.

* Changes in Tessera 0.1.0

** Display adapters for Gnus, mu4e, and Elfeed.
The global minor modes 'tessera-gnus-mode', 'tessera-mu4e-mode', and
'tessera-elfeed-mode' provide layouts for Gnus Summary, mu4e Headers, and
Elfeed Search buffers.  Native commands continue to handle reading, searching,
marking, and navigation.

Enabling an adapter configures existing and future matching buffers.
Disabling it restores their native display settings.  Loading a Tessera
library makes its settings available without enabling an adapter.

Tessera requires Emacs 30.1 or later and Alert 1.2 or later.  The adapters use
the Gnus included with Emacs, mu4e 1.14.3 or later, or Elfeed 4.0.1 or later.

** Single-line and two-line entry layouts.
Adapters initially display each entry on two visual lines.  Set the
buffer-local value of 'tessera-entry-layout' to 'single-line' for a compact
layout, or 'two-line' to restore the two-line layout.  Gnus and mu4e select
their thread layouts instead while native threading is enabled.

Subjects, contacts, feed titles, labels, dates, and state indicators have
separate layout roles.  Flexible fields shrink and optional fields can be
omitted in narrow windows.  Hover help provides details for abbreviated
content.  Horizontal spacing and fractional vertical padding are customizable.

** Current-entry and mouse highlighting.
'tessera-entry-current-face' highlights the entry containing point while
preserving native content faces.  Both content lines of an ordinary two-line
entry are highlighted; a thread heading is excluded from its message's
highlight.

'tessera-entry-hover-face' highlights individual elements under the mouse.
Decorative spacing and empty tree columns are not hover targets.

** Configurable state glyphs and thread connectors.
'tessera-glyph-style' selects ASCII, Unicode, or optional Nerd Icons glyphs.
The default is Unicode.  Graphical frames fall back to available glyph forms;
terminal frames use ASCII.  Thread connectors support Unicode and ASCII.

'tessera-glyph-color' selects semantic colors, an inherited foreground, or a
uniform color.  Backend glyph options allow individual indicators to be
changed or hidden.  'tessera-thread-glyphs' configures thread connectors, and
'tessera-entry-ellipsis' controls the truncation marker.

Customize and 'setopt' refresh affected displays.  The command
'tessera-refresh-glyphs' refreshes glyphs after direct assignments or changes
to their faces.

** Native thread layouts in Gnus and mu4e.
The adapters follow 'gnus-show-threads' and 'mu4e-search-threads', retaining
native thread ordering, reply relationships, and folding commands.  A thread
heading shows its subject and unread/total counts, followed by individual
message rows.  Counts include folded members of the current results; Tessera
does not fetch additional messages to complete the display.

Thread layouts have separate spacing around threads and between their
members.  Deep threads retain their branch columns and clip overflowing
content at the right edge.  When the usual position for point is clipped,
navigation uses the ellipsis until the window is wide enough again.

** Navigation follows native entry boundaries.
Adapted navigation commands leave point on the current entry when no target
is available.  Successful navigation places point at the subject in flat mail
layouts, the author or contact in thread layouts, and the title in Elfeed.
Elfeed's native 'n' and 'p' commands move by entries, including with numeric
arguments, rather than stopping on a second visual line.

Gnus retains the selection rules of the invoked command, such as visiting
only unread articles.  The option 'tessera-gnus-summary-boundary-navigation'
defaults to nil: native article navigation temporarily disables both
'gnus-auto-extend-newsgroup' and 'gnus-auto-select-next'.  A non-nil value
respects the individual values of those Gnus options; it does not enable them.

** Gnus article state and content indicators.
Summary rows show native article status, secondary marks, download
availability, and score, together with subjects, authors, dates, and labels.
Labels include registry marks, Gmail labels, and Keywords headers.

Attachment, signature, and encryption indicators use available header and
prepared-article MIME information.  Displaying these indicators does not fetch
articles.  Tessera neither verifies signatures nor decrypts messages.
Security indicators describe content presence, not verification success.

** mu4e message flags and pending operations.
Headers rows show subjects, senders, dates, labels, message flags, priorities,
and pending operations, including operation targets.  Labels and tags are
combined without duplicates.  Content indicators include attachments,
signatures, encryption, and calendar invitations when known to mu4e.

Native message-state faces remain part of the display.  Thread headings use
aggregate unread state while individual messages retain their own state.
Native fold summaries remain available with adjusted spacing.

** Elfeed entry metadata and tag faces.
Search rows show entry titles, feeds, dates, read state, and tags, with an
optional enclosure indicator.  Native title overrides and tag faces are
retained.  The unread tag is represented by its state indicator rather than
repeated among the textual tags.  Enclosure indicators use stored metadata;
they do not download enclosure files.

** Lisp interfaces for layouts and native metadata.
The shared renderer supports registered layouts, field width policies,
glyphs, padding, thread contexts, and navigation anchors.  Public native
metadata readers can be used without enabling display modes.

* Changes in Tessera X 0.1.0

** Experimental context snapshots.
The separate 'tessera-x' package provides read-only plain-text snapshots of
mail, news, and feed entries.  Snapshots contain source metadata and available
body text, with notes for missing or omitted content.  Mail and news are
grouped by message relationships, not merely equal subjects; feed entries are
grouped by feed URL.

Tessera X depends on Tessera but does not require enabling its display modes.
Load 'tessera-x-gnus', 'tessera-x-mu4e', or 'tessera-x-elfeed' to make the
corresponding options and commands available.  Native application libraries
are loaded when commands need them.  No experimental minor mode or default
key bindings are installed.

** Snapshots of selected messages and entries.
The commands 'tessera-x-gnus-prepare-context',
'tessera-x-mu4e-prepare-context', and 'tessera-x-elfeed-prepare-context'
collect the current selection from their native list buffers.

Gnus uses the active region, then process marks, then the article at point.
mu4e and Elfeed use native marks, then the active region, then the entry at
point.  Existing marks, pending operations, and read state are preserved;
downloading a Gnus body can update its native availability mark.

** Snapshots of the current subthread.
'tessera-x-gnus-prepare-subthread-context' and
'tessera-x-mu4e-prepare-subthread-context' collect the current message and its
descendants, including folded entries.  By default, selection stays within
the current results.  A prefix argument extends the search through the local
Gnus Agent overview or mu index.  Each backend's subthread-scope option can
make this local-index scope the default.

** Snapshots of today's local content.
'tessera-x-gnus-prepare-today-context' uses the current Summary group or the
group or topic at point in a Group buffer, including a topic's descendants.
'tessera-x-mu4e-prepare-today-context' uses the current Headers query or a
bookmark or maildir query at point in Main.  Its base query can be customized
with 'tessera-x-mu4e-today-query-function'.

'tessera-x-elfeed-prepare-today-context' uses the current Search filter or the
filter at point in a Tree buffer supplied by an Elfeed extension.

These commands select from local indexes or databases, not only visible
rows.  They do not download messages, fetch linked pages, synchronize mail,
or update feeds.  Missing local bodies remain identified in the result.

** Backend-specific body retrieval.
Gnus snapshots require Gnus Agent.  Selected-article and subthread snapshots
download missing bodies into the Agent by default.  Set
'tessera-x-gnus-body-policy' to 'local-only' to use cached bodies exclusively.
Today's snapshots always use cached bodies only.

mu4e snapshots read local message files.  Expanded subthread and today
queries use the local mu index and can finish asynchronously; they do not
initiate mail synchronization.

Selected Elfeed snapshots fetch linked HTTP or HTTPS pages by default and
fall back to stored feed content on failure.  The options
'tessera-x-elfeed-fetch-linked-content' and
'tessera-x-elfeed-fetch-minimum-characters' control whether to fetch a page.
Timeout and concurrency options bound active requests.  Retrieval does not
change the Elfeed database or download enclosure files.  Today's snapshots
always use stored feed content.

** Plain-text extraction and character budgets.
Mail extraction prefers a plain-text MIME alternative and otherwise converts
available HTML to text.  Attachment descriptions are retained as metadata;
attachment contents are not included.  Snapshots do not verify signatures or
decrypt encrypted bodies.

'tessera-x-context-max-characters' limits the rendered text budget to 900000
characters by default.  'tessera-x-context-body-max-characters' optionally
limits each body and defaults to nil.  Metadata and omission notices are
retained even if they exceed the total budget.  These are character budgets,
not token limits or limits on downloaded data.

** Snapshot management and result hooks.
Each source buffer owns its pending request and latest successful snapshot.
Starting another request cancels pending work without discarding the previous
successful result.  Killing the source buffer or changing its major mode
cancels pending work.

'tessera-x-show-context' displays the latest successful snapshot.
'tessera-x-cancel-context' cancels pending work, and
'tessera-x-discard-context' kills the latest snapshot buffer.

'tessera-x-context-ready-hook' receives the completed context for display or
other consumers.  User configuration can connect this hook to tools such as
gptel; Tessera itself does not send requests to language models.  Lisp
interfaces also support additional snapshot producers and asynchronous
completion.

